<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Madinat al-Zahra</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/936">936</a>–<a href="/years/1010">1010</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a> · Also known as Medina Azahara</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Madinat al-Zahra was the fortified palace city built from 936 by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abd-al-rahman-iii">Abd al-Rahman III</a> a few kilometres west of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cordoba">Córdoba</a> as the seat of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">Caliphate</a> of Córdoba. It housed the court, mint and administration in terraced halls and gardens. It was sacked in 1010 during the civil war that broke up the caliphate, and its ruins are now a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/world-heritage-site">World Heritage Site</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/madinat-al-zahra.jpg" alt="Madinat al-Zahra" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Kent Wang from Barcelona, Spain · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Madinat_al-Zahra_(52019329474).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Madinat al-Zahra, or Medina Azahara, meaning the radiant city, was a fortified palace-city on the western outskirts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cordoba">Córdoba</a>. Abd ar-Rahman III, a member of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/umayyad-caliphate">Umayyad dynasty</a>, built it, and the reason was chiefly politico-ideological. The Umayyads had been overthrown in the east by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abbasid-caliphate">Abbasids</a> in 750, and Abd ar-Rahman I re-established the dynasty in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-andalus">Al-Andalus</a> in 756, but its rulers governed as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emir">emirs</a>. Abd ar-Rahman III declared himself <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a> in 929, and the dignity of the title called for a new city, a symbol of his power that imitated the eastern caliphates and showed his superiority over his rivals, the Fatimid caliphs of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ifriqiya">Ifriqiya</a> and the Abbasid caliphs of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>According to historical sources the city was founded in 936, about 5 km west of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cordoba">Córdoba</a>, and Abd ar-Rahman's son and heir <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-hakam-ii">al-Hakam II</a> oversaw the work. The mosque was finished in 941 to 942 or 944 to 945, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a> was living there by 945, the road to Córdoba was paved in 946, and in 947 the apparatus of state, including the mint, moved there. Building continued through the reign of al-Hakam II (961 to 976).</p><p>The palaces housed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a>'s household and a large body of slaves, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eunuch">eunuchs</a>, mostly of European origin. The city held a throne hall, the Salón Rico, government offices, workshops making luxury objects, officials' residences and, on the lower levels, markets and districts for common workers. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-hakam-ii">al-Hakam II</a> there was a library said by some accounts to hold hundreds of thousands of volumes.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-hakam-ii">Al-Hakam II</a> was succeeded by his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hisham-ii">Hisham II</a>, aged 14 or 15, and real power passed to Ibn Abi Amir, who took the title al-Mansur, or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almanzor">Almanzor</a>. In 978 or 979 he began his own palace-city, Madinat az-Zahira, and when it was ready, in two years, he moved the government there, leaving Madinat al-Zahra in disuse. After al-Mansur's family lost power in 1009 and the civil war known as the Fitna began, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berbers">Berber</a> factions besieged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cordoba">Córdoba</a>, and Madinat al-Zahra was pillaged and left in ruins.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Discovery and study</h2><p>The city lies on the slopes of Jabal al-Arus, Bride Hill, in the foothills of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sierra-morena">Sierra Morena</a>, facing the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guadalquivir">Guadalquivir</a> valley, and its rectangular area of 112 hectares is laid out in three terraces. Excavations began in 1911, and only about 10 hectares have been excavated and partly restored, though they include the main palaces. An archaeological museum opened at the edge of the site in 2009, and on 1 July 2018 the site was listed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco">UNESCO</a> as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/world-heritage-site">World Heritage Site</a> under the name Caliphate City of Medina Azahara.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Madinat al-Zahra” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Workshops produced silk textiles, leather, paper, ceramics and carved ivory boxes such as the pyxis of al-Mughira, made at <b>Madinat al-Zahra</b> in 968.</blockquote><a href="/empire/caliphate-of-cordoba">Caliphate of Córdoba · Socioeconomy</a></li></ul></section>
